## 4. Things I believe the reviewer got wrong (NOT applied)

1. sr-a row 23 — the reviewer asks for "1,57 miliona TWD". This is a standing campaign exception
   (ACCEPTED-ROUND + R12) and a test pins the string; the row is not a Serbian defect but a product constraint.
2. sr-a row 31 / sr-b rows 15, 24, 29, 38, 70, 80 and sr-a rows 40, 48, 56, 59 — the reviewer repeatedly asks for
   small numerals to be spelled out. The requests are linguistically right, but `check-column-translation.mjs`
   only parses jedan/jedna/jedno, dva/dve, tri and their ordinals for `sr`. Every other spelled-out numeral deletes
   the digit from the target and fails the `numbers` check. I applied the spelling-out where the lexicon covers it
   (1, 2, 3) and kept digits elsewhere, per the work order's "prefer the form the checker accepts".
3. sr-b row 10 — "direktora" for 董事 in 017. Not applied as worded: 002/004/017 already use "član upravnog odbora"
   for the same organ, and one term per locale (R8) wins over a local preference. The garbled sentence itself is fixed.
4. sr-a row 18 — the reviewer supplies the author's aside "(Jesam li time odala koliko imam godina?)" as a full
   replacement for "Ti saloni ostaju deo sećanja na jedan raniji period Tajvana." I applied it as given (it matches
   EN/DE and the feminine *odala* is correct), but flag that this restores content that the Serbian file did not have
   — if the supervisor prefers the Serbian file not to carry the aside, revert that one line.
5. sr-a row 19 asks the *services page* to be changed to match 007's *lični kontakt*. That is a guidance-pack edit
   driven by a column row; I made it (guidanceContent.sr.pages.services), so the term is now identical in both
   surfaces. Noting it because it is the only row where a column review changed the pack.
